/// \defgroup grp_w1_profile_lib Profile library: ranges, timers and result writers
/// \ingroup grp_common_services
/// \brief EA's in-engine profiler from the Zero Hour libraries: named
///        ranges, a CPU clock calibration, its own allocator and the
///        result writers that run at exit.
///
/// The library lives in `Code/Libraries/Source/profile/` and is the Zero
/// Hour library of the same name, rebuilt on BFME 2's layouts. This group
/// covers its core: the Profile range API, the private allocator and clock
/// helpers, the exit hook, and the `profile` debug-command group
/// (ProfileCmdInterface). The high-level profiler (ProfileId,
/// ProfileHighLevel), the function-level tracer and the result-file
/// classes are not covered here.
///
/// **Ranges and frames.** Game code brackets work it wants measured with
/// Profile::StartRange and Profile::StopRange and a range name. Each
/// stop of an active range closes one *frame*, which gets a global frame
/// number and a name such as `newgame:3` (Profile::GetFrameName). The
/// measurements themselves are taken by the high-level profiler; this core
/// only decides when a range records and keeps the frame list. Range names
/// read at the direct call sites in the retail binary:
/// - `newgame`: around part of GameLogic's new-game setup;
/// - `crc`: around the deep CRC computation in GameLogic::update;
/// - `collision`, `logic` and `pathfind`: in other engine code.
///
/// One more StartRange and StopRange pair passes a null name, so it records
/// under the default name `frame`. The null is read from the register it
/// pushes, which that function only ever sets to zero.
///
/// **Recording is off by default.** A range records only when the last
/// entry of the pattern list whose wildcard pattern matches its name is
/// active. In BFME 2 an entry can also carry a frame budget, after which
/// it removes itself (Profile::StartRange, Profile::RemovePatternEntry).
/// The entries come from the `profile` debug commands `add` and `clear`
/// (ProfileCmdInterface). This guide describes both from the retail
/// BFME 2 handler, with Zero Hour's source and the help text in the
/// BFME 2 binary for comparison.
///
/// **Lifecycle.** Everything is set up by static initializers, before
/// `WinMain` runs. BFME 2's include the same four that Zero Hour's
/// profile.cpp defines, read here from the retail binary:
/// - allocate the single ProfileCmdInterface with ProfileAllocMemory and
///   construct it;
/// - register it with the debug library under the group name `profile`;
/// - store GetClockCyclesFast's result as the cached clock rate, which
///   also registers the built-in result writers;
/// - register ProfileShutdown with `atexit`, so the requested result
///   writers run when the process exits.
///
/// The built-in result writers, by registration name: `file_csv`,
/// `file_dot` and `file_gtt_dot` (GetClockCyclesFast). With none
/// requested, the CSV writer is requested at exit
/// (ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions).
///
/// **No BFME 1 comparison.** BFME 1's retail binary contains none of the
/// profile library's command or writer strings (not even `file_csv`), and
/// Open-BFME-1 has no profile library sources. Its only call into the
/// library, a `map_load` range in GameLogic, is compiled only in
/// `_PROFILE` builds, and that name is not in the retail binary either.
/// BFME 1 therefore gives no comparison for BFME 2's additions, which
/// include the `file_gtt_dot` writer, the `external` command, the pattern
/// frame budget and `spike` flag, the lowercasing of stored patterns, and
/// Profile::RemovePatternEntry. They are new relative to Zero Hour;
/// whether BFME 1's source already had them is unknown.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Nothing here is data-driven: no INI block, map script or UI movie
///   controls the profiler. The range names are string literals at their
///   call sites, and the result writers are fixed in code.
/// - The only control surface is the `profile` debug-command group
///   (ProfileCmdInterface). Commands reach it through the debug library,
///   whose console and start-up command file are described in
///   \ref grp_w1_debug_lib. They take the form
///   `profile.command arguments`, as in the `profile.result file_csv` that
///   ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions issues itself.
/// - In BFME 2 the `add` command lowercases the pattern when it stores it,
///   and the matcher lowercases uppercase ASCII letters in the range name,
///   so for patterns entered with `add`, matching is case-insensitive for
///   ASCII letters (Profile::StartRange, ProfileCmdInterface). Zero Hour's
///   `add` stores the pattern unchanged.
/// - The other side of that rule: a `clear` argument must be written in
///   lowercase. `clear` matches it against the stored patterns with the
///   same matcher, which lowercases only the stored side, so an uppercase
///   ASCII letter in the argument matches nothing (ProfileCmdInterface).
/// - The range functions themselves do not touch GameLogic state. They
///   update the profiler's tables and call the high-level profiler and two
///   function-level hooks that are null unless the `external` command has
///   attached an outside profiler (ProfileCmdInterface). Enabling a range
///   is therefore expected to change timing, not lockstep results (see the
///   frame-name limit under Profile::StopRange).
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The platform dependencies sit in a few small functions: Win32
///   `GlobalAlloc` and friends (ProfileAllocMemory, ProfileReAllocMemory,
///   ProfileFreeMemory), the x86 `rdtsc` instruction in inline assembly
///   (ProfileGetTime), and `timeGetTime` plus the performance counter
///   (GetClockCyclesFast). Apart from those, the range code needs the C
///   string functions, Win32 `wsprintf`, the debug library's
///   Debug::SimpleMatch, and the high-level profiler (ProfileId), whose
///   lock is a spin lock in inline assembly (see internal.h).
/// - Times are CPU time-stamp counts, converted with one rate measured at
///   startup. The conversion is only right when that counter runs at a
///   constant rate on every core the game runs on.
/// - The `external` command ties the library to Microsoft's Visual Studio
///   profiler: it runs a `profile.cmd` script from the game executable's
///   folder and loads `VSPerf80.dll` (ProfileCmdInterface). A port can
///   drop it without affecting the game.
///
/// \see \ref sub_common_services


/// \file Libraries/Source/profile/profile.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_profile_lib
/// \brief The profile library's main unit: the Profile range functions,
///        the library's allocator, the clock calibration and the exit hook.
///
/// Zero Hour's profile.cpp, rebuilt on BFME 2's layouts. Zero Hour's
/// version also defines the static objects whose initializers create and
/// register the command object, measure the clock and register
/// ProfileShutdown; for BFME 2's equivalents, see the group description.


/// \file Libraries/Source/profile/internal.h
/// \ingroup grp_w1_profile_lib
/// \brief The profile library's private header: its spin lock, the
///        allocator declarations and ProfileGetTime.
///
/// The library's main units include it. It pulls in the debug library
/// and the library's other internal headers, declares ProfileAllocMemory,
/// ProfileReAllocMemory and ProfileFreeMemory, and defines ProfileGetTime
/// and ProfileFastCS.
///
/// ProfileFastCS is Zero Hour's small spin lock, with a nested Lock class
/// that holds it for a scope. In retail BFME 2 only the high-level
/// profiler takes it (ProfileId, ProfileHighLevel); Zero Hour's
/// function-level profiler, when built in, uses it too. A thread that
/// finds it taken waits up to 1 ms on an event before retrying. A static
/// initializer creates that event before `WinMain`, and no code was found
/// that signals it, so the wait is in effect a 1 ms sleep. Zero Hour's
/// comment there says `SwitchToThread` was avoided because Windows 9x
/// lacks it.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Both ProfileGetTime and ProfileFastCS's atomic test-and-set are x86
/// inline assembly, which Microsoft's x64 compiler does not accept. A port
/// would replace them with compiler intrinsics or a standard mutex.


/// \class Profile
/// \ingroup grp_w1_profile_lib
/// \brief Static entry points for range profiling: start and stop named
///        ranges, list the recorded frames, read the CPU clock rate.
///
/// Profile is never instantiated: its constructor is private and declared
/// only, and every member is static. Its state is a set of process-wide
/// tables:
/// - one record per range name seen so far: the name, how many frames it
///   has recorded, whether it is recording now, whether the current frame
///   is to be appended to the previous one, the indices the high-level and
///   function-level profilers handed out for the current frame, and the
///   global number of its last recorded frame;
/// - the list of recorded frame names, indexed by global frame number;
/// - the pattern list that decides which ranges record (see the group
///   description). In BFME 2 a private helper, Profile::RemovePatternEntry
///   (a name chosen by the reconstruction), removes an entry whose frame
///   budget has run out;
/// - the CPU clock rate, filled in once at startup.
///
/// The range records and frame names grow as names and frames appear and
/// live for the whole process; none of the functions documented here frees
/// them. All of these tables are allocated with ProfileAllocMemory and
/// ProfileReAllocMemory, not the engine's memory manager.
///
/// ProfileCmdInterface is a friend; Zero Hour's `profile` debug commands
/// use that to edit the pattern list directly.
///
/// \note BFME 2's layouts differ from Zero Hour's in two places, both read
///       from the retail bodies. The per-name record has one more index,
///       whose role is unknown; Profile::StartRange clears it when
///       a range starts inactive. Each pattern entry has a frame budget
///       and a flag that the `add` command sets (see
///       ProfileCmdInterface).
/// \note Profile::StartRange's pattern test calls the shared wildcard
///       matcher that the ledger names Debug::SimpleMatch. Zero Hour's
///       Profile::SimpleMatch is recorded as folded onto the same body, so
///       whether BFME 2 kept a separate private matcher cannot be told from
///       retail. Unlike Zero Hour's matcher, BFME 2's lowercases uppercase
///       ASCII letters in the range name before comparing, but not in the
///       pattern. BFME 2's `add` command lowercases every pattern it
///       stores, so together the two make range matching case-insensitive
///       for ASCII letters.
/// \note Zero Hour also has AppendRange, IsEnabled, ClearTotals and a
///       Profile::AddResultFunction that forwards to
///       ProfileCmdInterface::AddResultFunction. Retail BFME 2 contains a
///       body shaped like AppendRange next to Profile::StartRange (see
///       Profile::StopRange), and a one-instruction forwarder to
///       ProfileCmdInterface::AddResultFunction next to
///       Profile::GetClockCyclesPerSecond, where Zero Hour defines
///       Profile::AddResultFunction. No call or address reference to either
///       was found, so neither appears to be used.
///
/// \see ProfileCmdInterface, \ref grp_w1_profile_lib


/// \fn void Profile::StartRange(const char *range)
/// \brief Begins a new frame of the named range, and starts measuring it
///        if the pattern list enables the range.
///
/// The usual pattern is a StartRange and Profile::StopRange pair with the
/// same name around the work to measure. GameLogic's new-game setup
/// opens `newgame`, and GameLogic::update opens `crc` around its deep CRC
/// computation. Calling StartRange on a range that is already recording
/// first stops it, which closes the previous frame.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The name is looked up with an exact, case-sensitive comparison. A
///   name seen for the first time gets a new record: frame count 0, not
///   recording, no previous frame.
/// - The record is marked as recording, in non-append mode, whether or not
///   the range turns out to be active. Profile::StopRange relies on that
///   flag.
/// - Every pattern entry is tested with the debug library's wildcard
///   matcher, Debug::SimpleMatch, in which `*` matches any run of
///   characters. The matcher lowercases uppercase ASCII letters in the
///   range name but not in the pattern, so an uppercase letter in a range
///   name matches only the lowercase letter in a pattern, and an uppercase
///   letter in a pattern matches nothing. In practice patterns never
///   contain uppercase ASCII letters: BFME 2's `add` command, the only
///   code that adds entries, lowercases each pattern before storing it (a
///   `_strlwr` call that Zero Hour's `add` does not make), so range
///   matching is effectively case-insensitive for ASCII. The last entry
///   that matches decides: active or inactive. No match means inactive.
/// - Active: if that entry has a positive frame budget, it is decreased by
///   one, and an entry whose budget reaches zero is removed with
///   Profile::RemovePatternEntry. A budget of zero or less means no limit.
///   Then ProfileId::FrameStart starts the high-level frame and its index
///   is stored; that index is -1 when the high-level profiler has no free
///   frame slot. ProfileFuncLevelTracer::FrameStart is called next, and its
///   result is stored as the function-level index. In BFME 2 that function
///   only calls an optional hook (see ProfileCmdInterface, command
///   `external`) and returns 1. Its name follows Zero Hour, whose
///   StartRange also calls ProfileFuncLevelTracer::FrameStart; the BFME 2
///   body, a single hook call, does not confirm the name by itself.
/// - Inactive: all three indices in the record are set to -1, so
///   Profile::StopRange records nothing.
///
/// \param range Range name, or null for `frame`. The name is copied when
///              it is first seen; the caller keeps ownership of its string.
///
/// \note Differences from Zero Hour: the frame budget, the extra index
///       cleared on the inactive path, the matcher's case folding (see
///       Profile), and the function-level call. Zero Hour makes that call
///       only in its `_PROFILE` builds, and there before the high-level
///       profiler starts; BFME 2 makes it after. The budget comes from the
///       `add` command: its help text in the BFME 2 binary describes an
///       optional count after which the pattern is removed.
///
/// \see Profile::StopRange, Profile::RemovePatternEntry, ProfileId::FrameStart


/// \fn void Profile::StopRange(const char *range)
/// \brief Ends the current frame of the named range and, if the range was
///        active, adds it to the list of recorded frames.
///
/// The partner of Profile::StartRange. Callers in BFME 2 close `newgame`
/// later in the new-game setup and `crc` in GameLogic::update.
/// Profile::StartRange also calls it to close a frame that is still open.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - An unknown name, or a range that is not recording, is ignored.
/// - The recording flag is cleared. Nothing else happens unless the
///   range's high-level index is valid, that is, the range was active and
///   the high-level profiler had a frame slot.
/// - In the normal case a new global frame is recorded: the range's frame
///   count goes up by one and the name `range:count` is appended to the
///   frame list (Profile::GetFrameName). In append mode, with a previous
///   frame to append to, the measurements are merged into that frame
///   instead. In Zero Hour, append mode is set only by AppendRange. Retail
///   BFME 2 contains a body of that shape next to Profile::StartRange, but
///   no call or address reference to it was found, so append mode appears
///   never to be entered.
/// - ProfileId::FrameEnd closes the high-level frame. Then
///   ProfileFuncLevelTracer::FrameEnd, which in BFME 2 only calls an
///   optional hook, is called when the function-level index is valid.
///
/// \param range Range name, or null for `frame`.
///
/// \warning The buffer for a frame name has room for a frame count of at
///          most five digits. Recording a range more than 99,999 times
///          writes past the end of that buffer. Zero Hour has the same
///          limit.
/// \note Zero Hour wraps the unknown and idle checks in `DFAIL_IF`, which
///       reports a failure in debug and internal builds and is a plain test
///       otherwise; retail BFME 2 has plain returns. In its `_PROFILE`
///       builds, Zero Hour also records a frame when only the
///       function-level index is valid, and closes the function-level frame
///       before the high-level one.
///
/// \see Profile::StartRange, Profile::GetFrameName, ProfileId::FrameEnd


/// \fn void Profile::RemovePatternEntry(PatternListEntry *entry)
/// \brief Unlinks one entry from the pattern list and frees it; used when
///        a pattern's frame budget runs out.
///
/// Zero Hour has no such function, and BFME 1 gives no comparison (see
/// \ref grp_w1_profile_lib). Its name was chosen by the reconstruction, as
/// retail has no symbol for it. Its only caller is Profile::StartRange,
/// which passes the entry that has just used its last frame.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The list is singly linked, so the function walks from the head to find
/// the link that points at the entry, unlinks it, and frees the pattern
/// string and the entry. It then rebuilds the list's tail pointer by
/// walking the list again, or clears it when the list is empty. In retail
/// both frees appear as direct `GlobalFree` calls; the pattern string's is
/// null-checked, the shape an inlined ProfileFreeMemory produces.
///
/// \param entry Entry to remove. It must be in the list.
///
/// \warning Nothing checks that the entry was found. An entry that is not
///          in the list makes the function dereference a null pointer.
///
/// \see Profile::StartRange


/// \fn unsigned Profile::GetFrameCount(void)
/// \brief Returns how many frames have been recorded so far, across all
///        range names.
///
/// Valid frame numbers for Profile::GetFrameName run from 0 to one less
/// than this. The CSV, DOT and GTT result writers loop up to it, to find
/// a frame by name or to write one column per frame.
///
/// \return The number of frames Profile::StopRange has recorded.
///
/// \see Profile::GetFrameName


/// \fn const char *Profile::GetFrameName(unsigned frame)
/// \brief Returns the name of a recorded frame, such as `newgame:3`.
///
/// Frames are numbered globally, across all range names, in the order
/// Profile::StopRange recorded them. The result writers use this to label
/// their output: ProfileResultFileCSV, ProfileResultFileDOT and
/// ProfileResultFileGTT all call it. ProfileResultFileGTT is the
/// reconstruction's name for a writer that Zero Hour does not have; retail
/// has no symbol for it. BFME 1's retail binary contains none of the
/// profile library's command or writer strings, not even `file_csv`, so
/// whether BFME 1 already had this writer is unknown (see
/// \ref grp_w1_profile_lib).
///
/// \param frame Global frame number, from 0.
/// \return The frame's name, owned by the profiler and valid until the
///         process exits; null when `frame` is not below
///         Profile::GetFrameCount.
///
/// \see Profile::StopRange, Profile::GetFrameCount


/// \fn __int64 Profile::GetClockCyclesPerSecond(void)
/// \brief Returns the CPU clock rate the profiler uses to turn time-stamp
///        counts into seconds.
///
/// The value is cached: a retail static initializer stores
/// GetClockCyclesFast's result in it once, before `WinMain`. The
/// high-level profiler's timed blocks divide their time-stamp differences
/// by this value.
///
/// \return CPU time-stamp counts per second, rounded to a whole MHz.
///
/// \see GetClockCyclesFast, ProfileGetTime


/// \fn __int64 GetClockCyclesFast(void)
/// \brief Registers the built-in result writers, then measures how fast
///        the CPU time-stamp counter runs.
///
/// Called once, by the retail static initializer that fills the value
/// Profile::GetClockCyclesPerSecond returns. Zero Hour registers its
/// result writers in this function too.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Three result writers are registered with
///   ProfileCmdInterface::AddResultFunction, each with a help string for
///   its optional arguments, which the `result` command lists:
///   - `file_csv`: ProfileResultFileCSV, no arguments;
///   - `file_dot`: ProfileResultFileDOT, optional file name, frame name
///     and fold threshold;
///   - `file_gtt_dot`: ProfileResultFileGTT (the reconstruction's name for
///     a writer Zero Hour does not have), optional file name, frame name
///     and a percentage threshold, in hundredths of a percent (10000 is
///     100%; the help string gives the default as 1%).
/// - The clock is sampled three times. Each sample busy-waits for the
///   multimedia timer to advance 2 ms, then counts time-stamp ticks
///   (ProfileGetTime) across a further 20 ms of that timer.
/// - Each count is scaled to one second with the performance counter's
///   measurement of the same interval. If the counter cannot be read,
///   the interval is assumed to be exactly 20 ms.
/// - The two samples closest to each other are averaged, and the result
///   is rounded to the nearest 1,000,000.
///
/// \return Time-stamp counts per second, rounded to a whole MHz.
///
/// \note Zero Hour registers only `file_csv` and `file_dot`, and passes
///       the CSV writer's factory for both; retail BFME 2 passes the DOT
///       writer's factory for `file_dot` and adds `file_gtt_dot`. Zero
///       Hour's function is file-local; retail does not show whether
///       BFME 2's is.
/// \par Remastering
/// The three samples busy-wait during static initialization for at least
/// about 60 ms in total (three 20 ms counts plus short waits for the timer
/// to tick). The wait is longer when the multimedia timer's resolution is
/// coarse. The result describes the time-stamp counter, which matches
/// real time only on hardware where that counter runs at a constant rate.
///
/// \see Profile::GetClockCyclesPerSecond, ProfileGetTime,
///      ProfileCmdInterface::AddResultFunction


/// \fn void *ProfileAllocMemory(unsigned numBytes)
/// \brief Allocates memory for the profile library from the Win32 global
///        heap.
///
/// All of the library's tables, frame names, result-writer objects and
/// the command object itself are allocated here or with
/// ProfileReAllocMemory. Callers include Profile::StopRange, the
/// high-level profiler and the result writers. Because it does not use the
/// engine's memory manager, it can run from static initializers, and its
/// allocations stay outside the engine's memory pools.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// One `GlobalAlloc` call with `GMEM_FIXED`, so the memory is not zeroed.
/// On failure the debug library's release-build crash path reports
/// "Debug mem alloc failed" (Zero Hour: `DCRASH_RELEASE`, which ends the
/// process).
///
/// \param numBytes Number of bytes to allocate.
/// \return The new block. Free it with ProfileFreeMemory.
///
/// \see ProfileReAllocMemory, ProfileFreeMemory


/// \fn void *ProfileReAllocMemory(void *oldPtr, unsigned newSize)
/// \brief Resizes a block from ProfileAllocMemory, with `realloc`-like
///        rules for null and zero.
///
/// Every call to it in the retail binary grows one of these tables, mostly
/// by one entry at a time:
/// - the range records, in Profile::StartRange;
/// - the list of recorded frame names, in Profile::StopRange;
/// - each high-level profile ID's array of per-frame values, in
///   ProfileId::FrameEnd;
/// - the result-writer registry, in ProfileCmdInterface::AddResultFunction;
/// - the command object's list of requested result writers, which the
///   `result` command grows each time it creates a writer;
/// - the GTT result writer's visited-edge list, which grows 1000 entries
///   at a time.
///
/// The CSV and DOT result writers do not call it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - A null `oldPtr` allocates `newSize` bytes with ProfileAllocMemory, or
///   returns null when `newSize` is 0.
/// - A `newSize` of 0 frees the block and returns null.
/// - Otherwise the block is resized in place with `GlobalReAlloc`. If that
///   fails, which the original comment says happens when a fixed block
///   grows, a new block is allocated, the smaller of the two sizes is
///   copied and the old block is freed. A failed allocation there reports
///   "Debug mem realloc failed" through the same crash path as
///   ProfileAllocMemory.
///
/// \param oldPtr  Block to resize, or null.
/// \param newSize New size in bytes.
/// \return The resized block, which may have moved; null when `newSize`
///         is 0.
///
/// \see ProfileAllocMemory, ProfileFreeMemory


/// \fn void ProfileFreeMemory(void *ptr)
/// \brief Frees a block from ProfileAllocMemory; a null pointer is
///        ignored.
///
/// A null check around `GlobalFree`. Callers include the result writers
/// and the `profile` command handler's `clear` command, which frees each
/// removed pattern and its entry this way, as Zero Hour's does (see
/// ProfileCmdInterface). In Profile::RemovePatternEntry and
/// ProfileReAllocMemory the free appears as a direct `GlobalFree` call.
///
/// \param ptr Block to free, or null.
///
/// \see ProfileAllocMemory, ProfileReAllocMemory


/// \fn void ProfileGetTime(long long &t)
/// \brief Reads the CPU time-stamp counter, the profile library's clock.
///
/// The high-level profiler's timed blocks read it when they open and
/// close and divide the difference by Profile::GetClockCyclesPerSecond to
/// get seconds; GetClockCyclesFast reads it to calibrate that rate. The
/// function is declared force-inline in the library's internal header,
/// and retail also has one out-of-line copy of it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Inline x86 assembly: `rdtsc`, with the two halves stored into `t`. The
/// two registers `rdtsc` overwrites are saved and restored around it.
///
/// \param[out] t Receives the 64-bit counter value. The header declares it
///               as `__int64`.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Inline assembly ties this to 32-bit x86 compilers; Microsoft's x64
/// compiler does not accept it. A port would read the counter with a
/// compiler intrinsic or switch the clock to the performance counter, and
/// then Profile::GetClockCyclesPerSecond must report that clock's rate.
///
/// \see GetClockCyclesFast, Profile::GetClockCyclesPerSecond


/// \fn void ProfileShutdown(void)
/// \brief Exit hook: closes any open high-level frames and runs the
///        requested result writers.
///
/// A retail static initializer registers this function with `atexit`, so
/// it runs while the process exits. It is the point at which profiling
/// results reach disk.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// ProfileId::Shutdown ends every high-level frame record still in use,
/// then ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions runs the result writers on
/// the single command object.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's version also shuts down the function-level tracer (in
///       its `_PROFILE` builds) and logs the CPU speed with `DLOG`, which
///       only debug and internal builds compile. Retail BFME 2 does
///       neither, and the log string is not in the binary, as expected for
///       a release build.
///
/// \see ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions, ProfileId::Shutdown


/// \class ProfileCmdInterface
/// \ingroup grp_w1_profile_lib
/// \brief The `profile` debug-command group, and the registry of result
///        writers that turn profiling data into files.
///
/// A command handler for EA's debug library (a DebugCmdInterface). It
/// holds two lists:
/// - a registry shared by the whole process, of result-writer factories,
///   each with a name and a help string for its optional arguments.
///   ProfileCmdInterface::AddResultFunction fills it; GetClockCyclesFast
///   registers the three built-in writers;
/// - per object, the result writers requested for this run. Each is
///   created from a registered factory and run at exit by
///   ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions.
///
/// There is exactly one object. One retail static initializer allocates
/// it with ProfileAllocMemory and constructs it; a second registers it
/// with the debug library under the group name `profile` (Zero Hour
/// likewise uses two statics).
/// Zero Hour builds it this way, in memory it never frees, so that its
/// destructor is never called; BFME 2 creates it the same way.
///
/// \par Commands
/// The group's help text in the retail binary lists six commands:
/// `result`, `caller`, `clear`, `add`, `view` and `external`. Zero Hour has
/// the first five; what follows for them is Zero Hour's behaviour plus the
/// BFME 2 help text, and for `add`, `clear` and `caller` also what the
/// BFME 2 handler does.
/// - `result` lists the registered writers with their argument help;
///   `result <name> <arguments>` requests a writer for exit.
/// - `add (+|-) <pattern>` appends a pattern that makes matching ranges
///   active (`+`) or inactive (`-`). BFME 2's help text adds an optional
///   count, after which the pattern removes itself (the frame budget in
///   Profile::StartRange), and an optional `spike` word, said to keep only
///   frames slower than the frame before. In BFME 2 the handler also
///   lowercases the pattern before storing it (Zero Hour stores it
///   unchanged), stores the count with `atoi` (with no count, the budget
///   is -1, meaning unlimited), and records whether a fourth argument
///   (`spike`) was given; it does not check that argument's text. What
///   `spike` does is described here only from the help text.
/// - `clear [<pattern>]` removes pattern entries; BFME 2's help text
///   mentions no argument. With no argument the pattern defaults to `*`,
///   which empties the list. With one, the handler removes and frees each
///   entry whose stored pattern text matches the argument under
///   Debug::SimpleMatch, where `*` in the argument matches any run of
///   characters. Stored patterns are lowercase (see `add`), and the
///   matcher lowercases only the stored side, so the argument must be
///   written in lowercase: an uppercase ASCII letter in it matches
///   nothing. After `add + GameLogic*`, `clear gamelogic*` removes the
///   entry and `clear GameLogic*` removes nothing. Zero Hour's `clear`
///   uses the same loop, but Zero Hour stores patterns unchanged and its
///   matcher compares exactly, so it does not have this restriction.
/// - `view` prints the pattern list.
/// - `caller` switches caller recording in Zero Hour's function-level
///   profiler, and Zero Hour compiles that body only when the profiler is
///   built in. In BFME 2 the handler accepts `caller` and returns success
///   at once: it reads no argument, changes nothing and prints nothing,
///   although its help text still describes turning caller recording on
///   and off.
/// - `external (+|-)` is not in Zero Hour. BFME 1's retail binary has
///   none of the profile library's command or writer strings, so it
///   cannot show whether BFME 1 had this command (see
///   \ref grp_w1_profile_lib). Its help text says it starts or stops an
///   outside profiler by running `profile.cmd` from the current directory
///   with `/start` (plus an output name and the process id) or `/stop`,
///   followed by any further arguments. The function that does this is
///   called right after the command handler compares the command with
///   `external`. Despite the help text, and the failure message that also
///   says "current working directory", it looks for `profile.cmd` next to
///   the game executable: it builds the path from the module file name,
///   not the working directory. It runs the script with the system
///   `cmd.exe`, from the Windows system directory, in a hidden window, and
///   waits up to 30 seconds for it. With no output name, a start passes
///   `profileOut`.
///   - If the script finishes within 30 seconds, a start loads
///     `VSPerf80.dll`, the Visual Studio profiler's API library, but only
///     while no library handle is saved, which in practice means until the
///     first successful load. It then points the function-level hooks that
///     ProfileFuncLevelTracer::FrameStart and
///     ProfileFuncLevelTracer::FrameEnd call at the DLL's `StartProfile`
///     and `StopProfile` exports. No other code was found that sets those
///     hooks to anything but null.
///   - If the DLL cannot be loaded, the start prints a failure message and
///     leaves the hooks unchanged; the handle stays empty, so a later
///     start tries again.
///   - A stop clears the hooks and frees the library, but it does not
///     forget the library handle, so a later `external +` does not reload
///     the DLL or set the hooks again.
///   - On a timeout, or when the script cannot be launched, neither mode
///     changes the hooks.
///
/// \see Profile, ProfileShutdown, \ref grp_w1_profile_lib


/// \fn void ProfileCmdInterface::AddResultFunction(ProfileResultInterface *(*func)(int, const char *const *), const char *name, const char *arg)
/// \brief Registers a result-writer factory under a name, so that the
///        `result` command can request it.
///
/// GetClockCyclesFast calls it to register the three built-in writers. The
/// only other call found in retail is the unreferenced forwarder of
/// Profile::AddResultFunction's shape (see Profile), through which Zero
/// Hour lets other code add writers.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// A null factory or a null name is ignored, and so is a name that is
/// already registered: the first registration wins. Otherwise the registry
/// grows by one entry (ProfileReAllocMemory) that stores the three
/// pointers. The strings are not copied, so they must outlive the process's
/// use of the registry; the built-in writers pass string literals.
///
/// \param func Factory that creates the writer from its arguments, given
///             as a count and an array (Zero Hour: the `result` command's
///             arguments after the writer name).
/// \param name Registration name, such as `file_csv`.
/// \param arg  Help text describing the optional arguments.
///
/// \see ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions, GetClockCyclesFast


/// \fn void ProfileCmdInterface::RunResultFunctions(void)
/// \brief Writes the profiling results at exit: if no writer was requested
///        it issues `profile.result file_csv`, then runs every requested
///        result writer.
///
/// Called only by ProfileShutdown.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - If no writer has been requested, the function issues the debug command
///   `profile.result file_csv` through the debug object. In Zero Hour that
///   requests the CSV writer, so an exit with no writer requested still
///   writes CSV results.
/// - Each requested writer then gets two virtual calls: WriteResults, to
///   write its file, then Delete, to free itself.
/// - The writer count is read from memory again after the debug command
///   and on every pass of the loop, as it must be: the command can add a
///   writer. Zero Hour's header warns that an optimizer bug requires this
///   field to be volatile.
///
/// \note Zero Hour issues the command through a static debug-library
///       function; BFME 2 calls a virtual function on the debug object.
///
/// \see ProfileShutdown, ProfileCmdInterface::AddResultFunction
